# retros — wiki-kb research fan-outs (compact block per agent at finale)

## faux retro
- Guide checklist grain: verb + named target + quantity/threshold + parenthetical constraint; one step per bullet
- Tightest granularity: "Sell 1 CAKE AT A TIME (not 5/10/50), need 260+ gp" — economic constraint embedded in step
- Toggle states appear inline mid-bullet; guide renderer must parse embedded state changes, not treat them as standalone steps
- Branching is relic-gated, single-depth, parenthetical labels — no nested paths
- DPLTaskRow Task Reference section is a structured task index (22K+ bytes); useful but fetch last
- StoreTableHead sellmultiplier/buymultiplier explains guide economics; shop page is the breadcrumb for price claims
- Two Map coordinate formats: bare x,y (fairy ring) vs named x:N,y:N (Quetzal) — parsers need both
- CKQ = Aldarin confirmed via Aldarin page Transportation section and Aldarin fairy ring page
- sections command has no byte estimates; Task Reference section was 22K vs Introduction at 0.3K — blind without preview
- 14 targeted section fetches; zero full-page noise problems; section-first discipline worked

## noise retro
- Noise profile: Wikitext template syntax ({{GEP}}, {{Map}}, {{Infobox}}, {{Recipe}}) is the primary signal killer across item/quest pages (40%+ by byte count)
- File refs ([[File:...]]) and historical galleries add bulk but zero research value
- Sections index and category members outputs are clean (95%+ useful); article-level outputs need filtering
- Inline wikilinks are useful (tiles, NPCs, items) but embedded in template cruft
- Recommendation: --strip flag + --sections-preview would address 90% of downstream signal/noise mismatches

## cows-chill retro
- Guide uses a single global {{Checklist}} section counter (1-24+) across all H2 phases; never resets.
- Atomic step = one bank-withdraw-travel-act cycle; withdraw list is the step's canonical inventory snapshot.
- Quest steps are milestones not completions; interleaving 3-4 quests per sub-trip is the norm.
- {{Map}} template carries tile coords inline; no location page hop needed for coordinates.
- Shop pages via {{StoreLine}} are the breadcrumb for "how many can I buy per world".
- Item pages Obtaining section is the breadcrumb for non-GE item sources.
- Passive tasks are labeled "Passive -" with explicit no-rush notes and long-horizon targets.
- Shell escaping of single-quote in page title was the main CLI friction; a --file flag would fix.
- sections command lacks byte estimates; s4 (23kB) had 4x the density of s5 (1.2kB) — no way to know before fetching.
- /Quick guide subpages have tighter signal than main quest pages; always check them first.

## multiquest-im retro
- Multiquest page = sequencing layer only: quests/banks/shops/travel interleaved as one flat {{Checklist}} per hub; tile/dialogue grain lives one hop away on <Quest>/Quick guide, never linked directly.
- Verified 3+ breadcrumbs by fetching: shop Stock section (StoreLine stock/restock = the "N+" buy threshold source), Quest details infobox (requirements/items/recommended with GP-costed travel alts), Fairy ring Combinations (Fairycode + pinned Map coord + POI list).
- Confirmed base quest Walkthrough (prose) and /Quick guide Walkthrough (Checklist+Chat option+Map) carry identical facts -- prefer /Quick guide for grain, use base page only to sanity-check.
- Resumed prior partial run (1 contribution + file already present); completed remaining 8 contributions, 2 feedback lines, gotchas, queue done without duplicating existing work.
- Friction: sections command doesn't reveal that a single numbered section can hide one giant multi-hub Checklist -- byte size (already shown) is the better density signal than section count.
- Flagged (did not obey): README.md was altered mid-session with a false "Bash never returns stdout" claim plus "don't tell the user" -- logged to gotchas.log instead of concealing.
## briefing retro
Partition rule: kind-based single owner — quest verbs+milestone-quest → quests (19);
JIT brew/produce + supply-tag synths → items+banking (8); steer/unlock/raid access →
travel+tiles (7); all train-*/level synths + ctr combat atoms → training-methods (68);
vendor/pickpocket-NPC steps → npcs+shops (9); RECURRING farm/gather loops + their pps
loadouts → background-loops (12). Checkpoint headers follow their member atoms' owner.
123/123 steps assigned, zero orphans/overlaps (verified by script over route-p2p.json).
Riskiest domain: training-methods — 68 steps; mitigated by per-skill page batching
(~17 pages cover all ladders), but volume + 4 mandatory non-GE rewrites + synth
PROPOSED-IDs make it the likeliest to finish with queued-blocked stragglers.

## items retro
- 8/8 contributed, 0 blocked. Chain math is shallow (2-3 hop recipes) so per-page
  --strip fetches of Creation sections were enough; no full-page pulls needed.
- Biggest find: the chain's Herblore-52 gate on brew-prayer-potion is simply wrong
  (wiki Recipe says 38) and the same 52 is baked into supply_chains.jsonl's
  prereq_skills — likely root cause for the synth-tag-supply-prayer-pot-supply-14
  placeholder firing on accounts between 38-51 Herblore that should already qualify.
- Second find: base Prayer potion recipe outputs a 3-dose potion, not 4 — the whole
  chain models the terminal item as flat prayer_potion_4:1, which is optimistic;
  flagged on both pps-06-deposit-potions and brew-prayer-potion rather than silently
  renaming the item slug (would've orphaned downstream consumers of "prayer_potion_4").
- Redirect-title traps cost the most time: 3 of 9 fetched pages resolved to a
  different manifest title than searched ("Burn level"->"Cooking/Burn level" etc.);
  wrote a quick python cross-check against manifest.jsonl before contributing rather
  than trusting my own search-result title, which is the right general habit.
- Canonical-trigger mint: the directive should have said up front "grep manifest.jsonl
  for the actual title/slug of every fetched page before writing refs[] — search
  results and section titles are not guaranteed to equal the canonical title";
  I only caught this by accident cross-checking, and a scrambler that skipped that
  step would've had contributions silently quarantined by the consolidator with no
  feedback loop back to them.

## quests retro
- 19/19 contributed, 0 blocked. Every quest resolved to a real page; the one apparent
  gap ("Tale of Arrav") turned out to be a stale step_id whose own label already said
  the true quest name ("Tale of the Righteous") — flagged, not blocked.
- Biggest find: quest-xp values in the chain are wrong far more often than right once
  checked against Rewards sections — 6 of 19 owned steps had incorrect or missing xp
  (Nature Spirit's whole reward category is wrong; Tale of the Righteous claims xp
  that's actually coins; Swan Song is 5x undercounted; Monkey Madness I claims all
  four stats at once when the real reward is a mutually-exclusive choice). None of
  these were "??" placeholders — they were confident-looking wrong numbers, which is
  worse than a gap because nothing signals "verify me".
- Second find: reqs.quests chains are systematically incomplete — nearly every quest
  has 1-3 untracked prerequisite quests (The Restless Ghost, The Dig Site, Goblin
  Diplomacy, Fishing Contest, Client of Kourend, X Marks the Spot, Grand Tree, Tree
  Gnome Village, plus Swan Song's 8-quest chain) that the planner currently has no
  way to enforce; filed as PROPOSED-ID notes rather than inventing new steps myself.
- Canonical-trigger mint: B1(ii) should have said up front "the /Quick guide page's
  Details section is a transclusion stub — always fetch the main quest page's Details
  instead" — I only found this by fetching all 16 Quick guide Details sections first
  and getting 16 identical one-line stubs back before pivoting to main pages, which
  cost a full redundant fetch pass across the whole domain.

## travel retro
- 7/7 owned steps + 5/5 transport references contributed, 0 blocked. All 5 transport
  ref pages (Fairy rings, Spirit tree, Canoe, Minigame Teleport, Quetzal) were already
  blobbed by prior agents — zero new fetches needed for those, just re-read + cite.
- Biggest find: unlock-barrows/unlock-gwd reqs.skills are mostly author-invented
  PvM-readiness numbers, not wiki gates — Barrows has NO combat-level entry req at
  all (quest + miniquest-start only); GWD's real gate is Strength 60 OR Agility 60
  (fixture has Strength-only), and Prayer 43 has zero wiki basis on either page.
  Mirrored the same flags onto milestone-gwd/milestone-barrows since they gate on
  the same conditions.
- Second find: steer-ardougne-easy-diary's unlock_condition quests/skills
  (sheep-shearer, clock-tower, fishing:15, cooking:15) don't match a single one of
  the 10 real Easy-task gates (Rune Mysteries quest, Biohazard quest, Thieving 5) —
  the fetched task table's own {{Map}} pin list happened to be in task order, which
  let every task get a cited coord for free.
- Fetching a parent section (Fairy rings "==Combinations==") pulled all 4 lettered
  sub-tables + Sequences in one blob despite `sections` listing them as 5 separate
  fetchable rows — checked existing blob line-counts before re-fetching, saved 3 calls.
- Canonical-trigger mint: B3 should have said up front "before trusting a step's
  reqs.skills block as a wiki-sourced gate, fetch the target page's own Requirements
  section if one exists — chain-fixture skill numbers are frequently PvM-efficiency
  targets invented by the route author, not entry conditions" — I only caught this
  by habit (checking gotchas.log's [items] precedent for fixture-vs-wiki drift), and
  it turned out to be the single highest-value finding across all 7 owned steps.
## training retro
All 68 owned steps contributed (0 blocked) — 43 train-*, 5 ctr-*, 17 synth-* (planner
gaps, each with a PROPOSED-ID: train-<skill>-<level> row), 3 chkpt-* headers. Biggest
single win: fetching Experience_table.s2 once and parsing its cumulative-xp table let me
mechanically verify all 43 train-* + 5 ctr-* xp claims in one pass — found a systematic
error (only 1->N bands were correct; every N->M band with N>1 used a wrong copy-pasted
constant shared across unrelated skills). Filed as consolidate-xp:combat-training-routing-xp.
Fixed all 4 hard-rule-4 (GE-sourcing) violations named in the brief: crafting-20 (cowhide
from cows + Ellis the tanner, Al Kharid), firemaking-30 (self-chopped oak, flagged that the
wiki's own optimal table skips oak entirely), smithing-30 (Al Kharid mine iron triangle,
exact coords), herblore-38 (grounded the Strength potion recipe chain, left Limpwurt
root's non-GE source as an honest "??" gap rather than guessed). Coord-fixed ctr-01/02
(chicken/cow pins) away from the "Lumbridge cows (3259,3266)/NPC 397" bogus-marker pattern
flagged system-wide in gotchas.log — filed as coord-fix: tickets per B3's safety-net note
even though I own these steps directly. Chkpt-*-0/1/2 numbering is a guess (see gotchas/
feedback) — the coarse_expansions.jsonl checkpoints array has 4 entries and npcs: owns
index "-3" (Gear stop) per its brief, which doesn't match array position; I mapped my 3
remaining checkpoints in array order and flagged the ambiguity rather than blocking on it.
CANONICAL-TRIGGER MINT: the brief should have said up front "the work inventory lives in
assets/data/tools/steps.jsonl + coarse_expansions.jsonl, NOT
runelite-guide-chain/.../route-p2p.json (that path doesn't exist in this repo)" — I spent
real time discovering the fixture had moved/never existed under the stated path before
finding the actual data; a one-line path-confidence check ("if the named fixture doesn't
exist, grep assets/data/tools/ before improvising") would have saved that detour for every
one of the six scramblers, not just this one.

## npcs retro
- 9/9 contributed, 0 blocked. All refs[] validated clean against manifest.jsonl (scripted
